WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.780
- What's up with you guys?

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:03.830
Thanks for coming through.

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:05.081
- [Murs] Thanks, man.

00:00:05.081 --> 00:00:05.914
- [Gary] Yeah.

00:00:05.914 --> 00:00:06.747
Where do you guys live?

00:00:06.747 --> 00:00:07.580
- [Murs] I live in Sherman Oaks.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.870
- [Jay] I live in Inglewood.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:09.703
- [Gary] That's great.

00:00:09.703 --> 00:00:10.830
Born and raised?

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.390
- [Murs] I'm born and
raised in mid-city LA.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.024
Both born and raised in LA.

00:00:14.024 --> 00:00:14.857
- [Gary] Awesome.

00:00:14.857 --> 00:00:15.690
Glad you guys came through.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:16.681
So what's been going on, anything good?

00:00:16.681 --> 00:00:18.590
- Just dropped an album

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.087
and waiting on my wife to
drop a baby any day now.

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:22.952
- [Gary] So everyone's dropping projects.

00:00:22.952 --> 00:00:24.370
- [Murs] Yeah, so I'm just
like looking at my phone like--

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:25.660
- Like every second, right?

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:26.860
I remember the first one too.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.530
My phone buzzed and I was like,

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.342
it was the only time my phone ring was on

00:00:30.342 --> 00:00:32.289
in the last 10 years.

00:00:32.289 --> 00:00:33.541
- [Murs] Yeah, exactly.

00:00:33.541 --> 00:00:34.779
- 'Cause I was so nervous.

00:00:34.779 --> 00:00:35.820
- [Murs] I'm so nervous to turn mine off.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:36.653
- I was so pumped.

00:00:36.653 --> 00:00:38.420
It happened on a Saturday.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.980
And how's the project doing?

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:41.830
- It's good, man.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:42.790
Good for an old rapper.

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.500
I'm out there, you know,

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.420
I talk about a loss on the album,

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.000
so it's kinda like a depressing,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.087
something that hasn't been done in rap.

00:00:50.087 --> 00:00:50.920
So a lot of people come out saying,

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.080
"Thank you for talking about it."

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.826
But I think a lot of
people are kinda like,

00:00:53.826 --> 00:00:55.770
"Ah, I don't wanna turn up to this."

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:56.603
- [Gary] Of course.

00:00:56.603 --> 00:00:59.760
Every project's got a different,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.130
you gotta speak your truth.

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:02.670
- Yeah, so I'm happy with it.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.320
I'm happy with my label.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.153
- [Gary] Supporting that.

00:01:05.153 --> 00:01:06.669
- Yeah, they knew where I was,

00:01:06.669 --> 00:01:08.051
and I was like if I come outta this hole,

00:01:08.051 --> 00:01:08.884
there's a record for you guys,

00:01:08.884 --> 00:01:09.717
this is what it's gonna be.

00:01:09.717 --> 00:01:10.550
And they're like...

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:11.383
- [Gary] Go do your thing.

00:01:11.383 --> 00:01:12.216
- Yeah.

00:01:12.216 --> 00:01:13.303
- [Gary] How about for you?

00:01:13.303 --> 00:01:15.053
- Me, I was just working with Nick.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:17.560
Our relationship's weird 'cause

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.700
I work in advertising.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.340
We just kinda came across each other

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.430
just working, what was it like, 2013,

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.100
we did a project together.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.850
And then I brought him on to do like

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:27.940
three or four more projects.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.112
And I've worked with a ton of artists.

00:01:30.112 --> 00:01:31.932
But working with him,
he just kinda gets it.

00:01:31.932 --> 00:01:33.991
You know, we just--

00:01:33.991 --> 00:01:35.186
- [Gary] How do you define gets it?

00:01:35.186 --> 00:01:36.620
Understands the end consumer?

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:37.903
- Yeah, he understands
the business side of it

00:01:37.903 --> 00:01:39.500
as well as the art side.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:40.914
Then just understands how
to work the clients as well,

00:01:40.914 --> 00:01:42.280
and knows what they're looking for.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.183
You know, outside of that,

00:01:45.183 --> 00:01:46.510
I think we've been trying to work together

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:49.285
outside of advertising, trying
to create our own content.

00:01:49.285 --> 00:01:51.350
He has a certain charisma about him

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.100
that goes beyond just rap.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.820
- OG in the Twitch game.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.417
- [Gary] I'm Aware.

00:01:56.417 --> 00:01:57.820
- OG in the Twitch game.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:58.653
I think he's got,

00:01:58.653 --> 00:02:00.830
he could definitely be a host.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:03.310
We did a Facebook live and
pop show for Boost Mobile

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.730
a couple years ago, on Where You At LA?

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.940
And he was essentially
the host of that show

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:09.213
and he brought in a bunch of artists.

00:02:09.213 --> 00:02:11.157
He's respected by--

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.173
- [Gary] Culture.

00:02:13.173 --> 00:02:14.408
- The most hip hop guys.

00:02:14.408 --> 00:02:15.484
- [Gary] Yep, I'm aware.

00:02:15.484 --> 00:02:18.497
- And like the most sensitive
females like his music.

00:02:18.497 --> 00:02:20.763
And so he appeals to a mass audience.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:25.030
He could stretch his
wings beyond just rap.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.870
Even though he's a fantastic rapper.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.020
- [Gary] And how are
you thinking about that?

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.150
Is that what you want or?

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:32.790
- That's just, that's what
I'm dealing with now, so.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.506
- [Gary] Trying to figure out that--

00:02:34.506 --> 00:02:36.235
- This was my last album
with the label so I'm free.

00:02:36.235 --> 00:02:37.494
- [Gary] Yes, which must feel interesting.

00:02:37.494 --> 00:02:38.375
- Yes.

00:02:38.375 --> 00:02:39.255
- [Gary] Options.

00:02:39.255 --> 00:02:40.088
- Yeah, exactly.

00:02:40.088 --> 00:02:41.727
I got gigs, like the people
that want me for personality,

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:43.110
"You should do a podcast."

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:46.325
I'm hosting HipHopDX, the biggest show,

00:02:46.325 --> 00:02:48.700
and I took them, to at one point

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.615
eight million views on one
video, took 'em 18 months.

00:02:51.615 --> 00:02:53.650
I did it in three months for them.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:55.510
Their subscribers have grown by 100,000,

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:56.832
so they want me to stay on,

00:02:56.832 --> 00:02:59.090
they want me to be the
content creator of all videos.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:00.350
And it's kinda like Joe Button.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:02.860
When Joe Button went public,
he had to kill his rap career,

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:04.530
which I think made sense

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:06.200
because everybody tries
to straddle the fence

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.620
seems to get lost.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.470
People on the hosting--

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:10.756
- Why do you think he did that?

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:12.150
You think he loved it or
he just thought it was

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.930
economically the right move for him?

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:15.010
- I think economically and I think

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:15.980
he's into being a dad now.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:19.675
You wanna make money at rap
you gotta be everywhere,

00:03:19.675 --> 00:03:20.690
you gotta go around, shake hands,

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:22.510
you gotta stand in VIP, you gotta,

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.890
and then you gotta deal
with a lot of flack.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:28.490
I put out my album professing my life

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:30.123
and Pitchfork gave it a 6.8.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:33.160
It's like, fuck, you know like, right.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.370
But when I create these content

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:36.650
I'm talking about other rappers

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:37.820
and putting together these words

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.090
and the advertisers are
streaming video games,

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:41.778
I don't deal with as much,

00:03:41.778 --> 00:03:43.300
you know, it's not my heart.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.870
It's what I love doing it
'cause I love the culture,

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:47.367
but I'm not so invested in it,

00:03:47.367 --> 00:03:49.420
and I don't have to be on the road

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:50.650
100 days, 200 days outta the year.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.400
- [Gary] I totally understand.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.010
- But it's still a good job.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:55.720
Like if my rap album wasn't selling

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.787
and I wasn't getting offers
to do tours and gigs,

00:03:57.787 --> 00:04:00.117
I would have dropped it
and stayed at HipHopDX

00:04:00.117 --> 00:04:01.263
but it's still like,

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:04.820
You know, I don't know which way to go.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:06.230
- [Gary] And what do
you do in advertising?

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:07.733
- I work at social.

00:04:07.733 --> 00:04:09.590
- [Gary] Where?

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:11.161
- I work at MullenLowe now,

00:04:11.161 --> 00:04:12.993
I was at 180 LA before that.

00:04:12.993 --> 00:04:14.440
Where he's working.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.070
- [Gary] You've been in the
agency world for a long time?

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:18.915
- Yeah, four almost five years now.

00:04:18.915 --> 00:04:20.432
- [Gary] And before that?

00:04:20.432 --> 00:04:21.707
- I was working in music.

00:04:21.707 --> 00:04:23.456
I was a DJ, and I ran
a small record label.

00:04:23.456 --> 00:04:25.948
Kinda doing stuff that
lead to advertising.

00:04:25.948 --> 00:04:28.372
Just 'cause I already had
a, working on the internet.

00:04:28.372 --> 00:04:30.039
- [Gary] I've heard.

00:04:31.467 --> 00:04:33.339
- So when I got in, it was
just basically Twitter.

00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:36.816
They hired me 'cause they needed someone

00:04:36.816 --> 00:04:38.680
to run Boost Mobile social account.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.323
They needed someone to do marketing,

00:04:40.323 --> 00:04:41.669
the knew (mumbling).

00:04:41.669 --> 00:04:43.325
So I just kinda fell into it.

00:04:43.325 --> 00:04:47.963
And before I knew it it just
kinda, worked my way up.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:51.119
- [Gary] I love it.

00:04:51.119 --> 00:04:52.423
- This game's crazy.

00:04:52.423 --> 00:04:54.070
Me going out, going to
meetings with him and then

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:55.720
this person gets poached by this agency,

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:56.720
next year they're working here,

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:57.887
then a year they're working
back at this agency.

00:04:57.887 --> 00:04:59.853
I'm like, oh you mother fuckers are just,

00:04:59.853 --> 00:05:02.423
ad game's wild.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:04.738
- [Gary] I agree.

00:05:04.738 --> 00:05:05.571
- Come to a meeting here,

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:06.410
we're having this party
Friday night, come to this.

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:08.040
I'm like didn't he used to work here,

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.720
and you used to work, and
everybody knows each other,

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.850
but they all...

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:12.700
- [Gary] I agree.

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:16.967
'Cause they don't have to sign
contracts that lock them in.

00:05:16.967 --> 00:05:17.900
(laughing)

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.858
- [Murs] That's why it's so bizarre.

00:05:19.858 --> 00:05:21.608
You were just on Def Jam yesterday.

00:05:24.002 --> 00:05:26.230
- I upload, it was weird as
hell to me when I first started.

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:28.139
You're leaving already?

00:05:28.139 --> 00:05:29.195
You've only been here like six months.

00:05:29.195 --> 00:05:30.353
That's how, in this industry,

00:05:30.353 --> 00:05:31.186
that's how you work your
way up is just like,

00:05:31.186 --> 00:05:35.160
they're not really gonna promote
you internally that quick.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.550
If you wanna just like take
that little tiny steps.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:42.400
The agencies aren't advancing
people that quickly.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.060
- I couldn't agree more.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:45.580
It's the biggest thing I noticed

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:47.670
when I first started Vayner.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:50.310
I didn't realize when
people would tell me,

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:51.394
I don't know--

00:05:51.394 --> 00:05:52.227
- It's a margin game, right?

00:05:52.227 --> 00:05:53.340
- Yeah, that's right.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:56.620
All the agencies, the company
you work for right now

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:58.783
is a huge publicly traded company.

00:05:58.783 --> 00:06:00.570
They don't give a fuck about you.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:02.279
You're a line item.

00:06:02.279 --> 00:06:04.700
I've said this before,

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.310
I don't know if you've ever
heard me say this, Judy,

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:07.540
and this is the truth.

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:12.020
If I actually understood the
advertising industry's dynamic

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:14.120
between employees and holding companies,

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:15.530
I would have never started VaynerMedia.

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:16.690
That's real life.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:19.430
'Cause I hate when
people leave my company.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:20.263
I hate it.

00:06:20.263 --> 00:06:21.870
I love continuity.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:23.510
You even know the way I talk when we,

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:25.610
like I'm really trying to figure out

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:27.060
how to make it forever,

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:29.310
or at least a long, long, long time

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:32.029
so that when it happens it feels like

00:06:32.029 --> 00:06:34.650
cool and awesome and right,

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.807
not, "Oh I need to be
at a place for 18 months

00:06:36.807 --> 00:06:37.640
"and then leverage."

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.650
And now because our brand is so strong,

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:41.670
a lot of the kids do think I know

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:43.840
that are coming in this
is a great launching pad

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:45.690
because this is a good brand right now.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.470
And you know the more traditional shops

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:51.840
want kids that we're grooming.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.963
People get caught up in short
term thinking, you know?

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.963
People just, people are
confused by abundance.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.130
People think that they
need to take something

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.230
that somebody else is gonna get

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.860
without realizing there's so
much for everybody to eat.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.060
Tonight America's gonna
throw out more food,

00:07:10.677 --> 00:07:13.833
food that America throws out tonight,

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:17.870
could feed so many parts of the world,

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:20.345
we just don't think, we
think of things in silos.

00:07:20.345 --> 00:07:22.600
There's so much more to go around.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:24.560
And everybody's grabbing 'cause they think

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:26.334
that's the only, like it's all around us,

00:07:26.334 --> 00:07:28.700
and I just watch it.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:31.080
And I'm like, I never think
that somebody's victory

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.270
is at my expense.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:33.901
If I was a rapper, like literally,

00:07:33.901 --> 00:07:37.060
if whoever has the biggest
song in the world right now,

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:39.180
I would never think oh my God,

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:43.328
that dude or that woman is
getting all the listens.

00:07:43.328 --> 00:07:44.270
(laughing)

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:45.420
Like, you know what I mean?

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:46.253
- [Murs] That's how people act.

00:07:46.253 --> 00:07:47.150
- That's how people act.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:49.550
Like, you know, just talking
about current hip hop.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:52.970
I don't, I would hope that Nicki Minaj

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:54.627
or the people around her
don't sit there and say,

00:07:54.627 --> 00:07:57.520
"Well, Cardi B's taking our listens."

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:00.360
I understand that's an
easy, lazy thing to say

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:01.610
if you're not thoughtful.

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:05.080
If Nicki Minaj puts out a
fucking fire track right now,

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:06.645
people are gonna listen.

00:08:06.645 --> 00:08:07.478
- [Tyler] 'Cause yeah, it doesn't matter.

00:08:07.478 --> 00:08:09.010
- There's abundance.

00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:10.870
- [Tyler] Especially
now that music is free.

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:12.003
- There's abundance.

00:08:13.128 --> 00:08:14.457
- [Murs] Yeah, I don't understand it.

00:08:14.457 --> 00:08:18.010
- But that's the confusion
in the system, you know?

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:22.560
And so people need a
calibration of understanding

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:24.200
how much food there is to eat,

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:26.330
and I think everybody needs more empathy.

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:30.710
To me that's so stuck in
my right here right now.

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:31.710
You starting with a new label.

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:33.200
You're gonna be a different
version of yourself

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:34.650
than if it's the third label,

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:36.690
third album with a label,

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.950
because you may for your
own ambition and skillset

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:43.380
wanna prove something, or
start off the right way.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.740
So there's so much context

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:48.623
in every interaction,
in every transaction.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:52.763
That's what I spend all
my time on, empathy.

00:08:54.458 --> 00:08:55.430
- [Murs] That's rare.

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:56.790
- I think so, but that's also why I think

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:58.320
I have rare results.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:08:59.940
Basically it's a very
basic business thing,

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:01.830
if you can survive doing it

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:03.738
the way that nobody else is doing it,

00:09:03.738 --> 00:09:05.700
if you have the ability,
and this is what I have,

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:09.700
to sell it to keep you
